WEBVTT

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:05.329
Every bend to reach a box costs a little energy.

00:00:05.609 --> 00:00:09.067
Over a shift, that adds up to aching backs and slower work.

00:00:09.667 --> 00:00:12.790
Flat shelves hide what is inside bins and boxes,

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:16.303
and fixed heights force operators to stretch or stoop.

00:00:16.583 --> 00:00:21.794
Shorter and taller operators are affected most, because one height never suits everyone.

00:00:22.394 --> 00:00:28.993
A variable-angle shelf can sit flat, or tilt to 5, 10, 15, 20 or 25 degrees,

00:00:28.993 --> 00:00:32.119
so bins and boxes face the operator.

00:00:32.399 --> 00:00:37.245
It can be hooked at any height on the frame, to suit the operator, the box or the task.

00:00:37.525 --> 00:00:41.124
Adjust it whenever the box size or the operator changes.

00:00:41.724 --> 00:00:46.051
Furnisys variable-angle shelves carry 68 kilos UDL.

00:00:46.331 --> 00:00:51.131
A shirt maker uses them on Link trolleys so cartons are loaded at standing height.

00:00:51.411 --> 00:00:56.309
A gas-detector maker angles a laptop shelf for comfortable firmware testing.

00:00:56.909 --> 00:00:59.802
Bring the work to the operator, not the other way round.

00:01:00.082 --> 00:01:01.683
Where do your people bend the most?

00:01:01.963 --> 00:01:06.936
Comment below, and next, compare instrument risers and adjustable riser shelves.

00:01:07.536 --> 00:01:11.781
Furnisys: complete workspace systems, planned around your process.

